        "confidence": 0.9
      },
      {
        "text": "The speaker suspects a connection between the desire to hold people to account and the ambition to become a Senator.",
        "confidence": 0.87
      }
    ],
    "assumptions": [
      {
        "text": "Public education through hearings requires intentional design and purposeful use.",
        "confidence": 0.94
      },
      {
        "text": "Political ambition influences motivations behind calls for accountability.",
        "confidence": 0.91
      },
      {
        "text": "Accountability efforts should serve broader civic purposes like informing citizens.",
        "confidence": 0.89
      },
      {
        "text": "There is a plausible link between personal political aspirations and public-facing advocacy.",
        "confidence": 0.86
      }
    ],
    "turn_type_label": "Substantive",
    "conversation_move_label": "Clarification Request (Specific)"
  },
  {
    "episode_id": 92669,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/9/2/92669.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 9,
    "turn_text": "It's definitely true that in the Senate, you get to serve on twice as many committees. You have a larger staff. You have a broader reach of policy issues you work on. So I think twice as many opportunities to whiteboard those in power who need to be held to account. But I think in general, in both the House and the Senate oversight is one of the most important responsibilities we have. And we often don't do it. And when I think about oversight, I think about being a watchdog for consumers, for employees, for taxpayers. I think about trying to close the gap between what politicians announced at a press conference that they've accomplished and then what really happens in people's lives. And when I got to Congress four and a half years ago, it really surprised me that not everybody was as committed to oversight as I was. But I think when you look under the hood of our government at the campaign contributions, at the lobbyist donations, at the fact-finding trips to the French Riviera, you understand that there are too many elected officials whose priorities are being shaped by their donors and by institutional forces in Washington, rather than being focused on what families need from them.",
    "speaker_id": "SPEAKER_00",
