  },
  {
    "episode_id": 92669,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/9/2/92669.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 17,
    "turn_text": "In the Senate, it's pretty clear that we're going to need to rethink and eliminate the filibuster so that we don't have senators hiding behind the filibuster to avoid taking votes on where they stand on our most fundamental freedoms. the American people support the right of each person to make their own decision about whether to have an abortion in consultation with a partner, a doctor, a family member. And that is where the American people are. And so what we've seen here is the far right near the tail end of a 30, 40 year war on reproductive freedom. And so I think the answer here is that we all need to make this a top issue on the ballot box. We need to elect people who will not just support a right for an abortion, but will reform our Senate, will challenge institutional power in Washington so that our Senate actually stands up and takes the votes on the issues, on the protection, on the freedoms that are most sacred to us.",
    "speaker_id": "SPEAKER_00",
    "inferred_speaker_name": null,
    "inferred_speaker_role": null,
    "chosen_text_key": "transcript",
    "explicit_propositions": [
      {
        "text": "The filibuster needs to be rethought and eliminated in the Senate.",
        "confidence": 0.97
      },
      {
        "text": "The American people support individual autonomy in decisions about abortion.",
        "confidence": 0.96
      },
      {
        "text": "Senators sometimes use the filibuster to avoid taking votes on fundamental freedoms.",
        "confidence": 0.95
      },
      {
        "text": "There has been a decades-long conservative campaign against reproductive freedom.",
        "confidence": 0.94
      },
      {
        "text": "Reproductive rights should be a top priority in elections.",
        "confidence": 0.93
      },
      {
        "text": "The American public believes individuals should consult partners, doctors, and family when deciding about abortion.",
        "confidence": 0.92
      },
      {
        "text": "Elected officials should actively support abortion rights and work to reform the Senate.",
        "confidence": 0.91
      }
    ],
    "assumptions": [
      {
        "text": "The current Senate structure obstructs democratic accountability on core moral issues.",
        "confidence": 0.95
      },
      {
        "text": "Abortion is a fundamental personal liberty deserving constitutional or political protection.",
        "confidence": 0.94
      },
      {
        "text": "Public opinion reliably reflects ethical truth regarding reproductive rights.",
        "confidence": 0.93
      },
      {
        "text": "Electoral pressure can effectively compel institutional change in Congress.",
        "confidence": 0.92
      },
      {
        "text": "Institutional power in Washington systematically undermines popular will.",
        "confidence": 0.9
      },
      {
        "text": "Challenging the filibuster aligns with broader democratic legitimacy and responsiveness.",
        "confidence": 0.89
      }
    ],
    "turn_type_label": "Substantive",
    "conversation_move_label": "Assert / Elaborate"
  },
  {
    "episode_id": 92669,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/9/2/92669.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 18,
    "turn_text": "Well, speaking of the Senate and the filibuster, the other big headline I wanted to ask you about was how Senator Dianne Feinstein said on Wednesday that she'd ask and Democrats to replace her on the Judiciary Committee while she recovers from shingles so that they can continue to do their work and confirm Biden's Justices, meanwhile, your colleague, South Park Congressman Ro Khanna has called for her resignation. I'm wondering, do you think Diane Feinstein should step down ahead of the end of her term, given the fact that there are some real questions about whether or not that replacement would be allowed to go forward smoothly?",
