  {
    "episode_id": 92669,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/9/2/92669.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 54,
    "turn_text": "Like a lot of parents, I use organization to try to balance and juggle all of the responsibilities I face. So in my case, that means things. And I've gotten better at this, by the way, as I've been in Congress longer, but I've really had to come up with it on my own because there hasn't been anyone else in my situation. But for example, it's things like pre-making the meal. So last night I sliced up the peppers and the onions and I got all the ingredients out and I walked my kids through how to cook it. And we'll see if it turns out tonight. I asked them to send me a picture if it was edible and they have a backup plan to go to In-N-Out Burger if they fail at making dinner. I do have a number of childcare providers who stay with the kids when I go to Washington. I live in California. That is, I come back every opportunity I can. I think seeing what's happening on the ground in California, talking to Californians. My kids go to California public school. My son's going to be applying to the California university system. really keeps me grounded on what we are facing and what we need from Washington. So I'm very grateful to all of the neighbors and childcare providers and to my kids who frankly step up and help a lot. My middle son Paul, we call him LB for laundry boy. He does all of the laundry. My daughter Betsy unloads the dishwasher. That's one of her responsibilities. My son Luke takes the garbage and recycling out. So we all pitch it and do it together, but it definitely takes a lot of hours in the day. And like most working Americans, and particularly those of young parents, I think you just kind of keep trying to put one foot in front of the other and make it to the end of the day and get up tomorrow with enough energy to keep going.",
