  {
    "episode_id": 92669,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/9/2/92669.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 72,
    "turn_text": "This is a terrific question. Thank you so much for giving me the chance to talk about it. One of the things that I've brought to the foreign policy work that I've done is the same commitment to oversight that you have seen with regard to how I address issues like big, what big farm is doing, what big banks are doing, what big oil is doing. The truth is we also have a big defense industry. And it, too, gets its way in Washington through corporate-packed donations, through lobbyist donations. I don't take that money, and I'm not bought and paid for. So last Congress, and in the past, I have voted no on the National Defense Authorization Act, because I think that budget does not reflect kind of what we actually need to keep our men and women safe who are serving us. I'll give you a great example. This last Congress, the Pentagon and the White House, came to Congress and said, we need X dollars. This is what the general said we needed. This is what the White House said we needed to keep our world secure. And the House Armed Services Committee went and added 10% to the tune of tens of billions of dollars to top off that budget. I voted no because that is not about keeping our country safe. That is about lining the pockets of the defense industry. I've done a lot of work on oversight with regard to the United States nuclear legacy, for example, in the Marshall Islands. I have a particular interest in South China, the South China Sea, and the increasing contest that we're facing, how the United States can work more closely with Australia as our ally. to guard against that increasing power from China. Recently made my first trip to Israel, and then also at Thanksgiving, had the chance to visit six different countries and serve Thanksgiving meals to our troops, visiting Kuwait and Lebanon, among others, Iraq, and got a chance to see firsthand why it is really, really important that when we make policy in Washington, we take the time to talk to and learn from those who are on the ground. trying to deliver American policy into people's lives.",
