  {
    "episode_id": 92669,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/9/2/92669.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 81,
    "turn_text": "will happen to them? I guess we'll celebrate. I guess we'll continue to be their mother or I'm not going to continue to be there for them and they're going to continue to be a wonderful family to me. So I do think that those questions used to rub me the wrong way. And then I started to listen to kind of what was behind the questions. And it is sometimes coming from a place of sexism or sort of doubting women's ability to perform at the highest levels of government. Sometimes I hear those questions coming from other parents, from moms, who say, I'm struggling. I work 22 miles away and I can't get to childcare on time and I can't afford to, you know, the thought of trying to help my, get dinner on the table, the thought of trying to get all the forms filled out for summer camp is overwhelming to me as a working parent. It must, you know, what's it like for you? And so I think it's important to show people in the book that yes, single parents, yes, parents of young children can can and must serve in our Congress, but that's also going to look a little bit different than how the job is traditionally done. Some of my colleagues go out after votes, they'll go out to dinner or go out to drink whiskey together. I go home to FaceTime my kids and to talk with them about how their day was and find out if they managed to make dinner and figure out what I need to do for tomorrow to have them be successful in school. I think I try to be honest about it in part because I want to quiet those doubts that Cecile is absolutely right that people have. She's definitely right that women in politics face different questions. I talk a little bit in the book. I swear about how people think about my appearance and how they criticize it. I talk a little bit in the book. about and we talked a little bit in this interview about how people think about women as leaders and women as bosses, right? The kind of tired old trope that men have high expectations, but women are unreasonable. And I think it's important to talk about why those are wrong concepts. And so in this book, one of the things I tried to do, you're absolutely right is be honest about the challenges of being a single mom, not to not to turn people away from it, but to actually show that it is possible. And it matters in how we make policy. It matters in the fights I take on. It matters in how I see problems and it matters in how hard I fight to get solutions.",
    "speaker_id": "SPEAKER_00",
    "inferred_speaker_name": null,
    "inferred_speaker_role": null,
